          "text": "{\"translation_en\":null,\"larger_context_summary\":\"Near the end of the work, Franco addresses Nikki directly in an emotionally charged marriage proposal. He describes her as transformative—someone who has changed his life, makes him a better man, and has become essential to the future he imagines—before asking her to marry him. The passage clearly presents them as romantic partners, although the supplied context does not establish the proposal’s outcome or the specific events that led to this moment. The immediate stakes are both emotional and relational: Franco is disclosing the depth of his attachment while asking Nikki to make a lasting commitment.\",\"interpretive_commentary\":\"The target expression functions primarily as a sincere-seeming claim about Franco’s enduring emotional state. Its truth-presenting force is strengthened by the elaboration that follows: Nikki has transformed his life, improved him, and become inseparable from his imagined future. The superlative phrasing—“more than words could ever express”—and the description of his emotional voice increase the declaration’s intensity, but intensity alone does not make it an expressive or reflexive outburst. Franco’s speech is organized, purposeful, and directed toward a consequential question. The marriage proposal gives the avowal some relationship-shaping significance, since it helps justify and frame the requested commitment. Even so, the words of love do not themselves create that commitment; the explicit proposal performs the principal interpersonal action. This makes the occurrence a useful example of a strong avowal embedded in a partly performative conversational turn.\"}"
